{% comment %}
  The brand palette -> --suf-* tokens.

  Five colours, from the client brand guide. These are the only colour values
  anyone edits; everything else in frontend/styles/_tokens.scss is derived from
  them semantically (--suf-color-link, --suf-cta-bg and so on), so a component
  never names a colour, only a role.

  Edited at: Theme settings > SUF Brand Palette.

  Deliberately separate from the legacy Appearance pickers. Those describe the
  inherited theme, where "Primary Color" is red. In this palette navy is
  primary and red is an accent, so one stored value could not serve both --
  sharing them would have baked a lie into the token names.

  Rendered after suf.css so it overrides the compiled fallbacks. Every value has
  a `default:` so a blank setting cannot emit an empty custom property, which
  would be invalid at computed-value time and unset the declaration.
{% endcomment %}
<style>
  :root {
    --suf-navy: {{ settings.suf_color_navy | default: '#003049' }};
    --suf-blue: {{ settings.suf_color_blue | default: '#0292db' }};
    --suf-red: {{ settings.suf_color_red | default: '#c1121f' }};
    --suf-red-dark: {{ settings.suf_color_red_dark | default: '#780000' }};
    --suf-grey: {{ settings.suf_color_grey | default: '#dadada' }};
  }
</style>
